{
  "_title": "Methalox first stage: split jacket, extension circuit and turbine exhaust film",
  "_comment": "A 500 kN LOX/LCH4 first-stage engine at 10 MPa, MR 3.5 and area ratio 25, with three cooling features in series along the wall. The chamber jacket is a single counterflow pass (architecture A) to an area ratio of three: 28 kg/s of methane from 20 MPa and 115 K in 120 circular GRCop-42 channels, which split to 240 at an area ratio of two over four channel diameters. It spends 0.79 MPa, leaves at 289 K into the outlet volute, and holds the hot wall to its 800 K limit. The extension circuit (architecture D) cools the next stretch, from the joint at three to twelve, on two passes of its own: 4 kg/s of methane from 20 MPa and 115 K runs aft down 160 channels and back up 160, leaves at 543 K after 6.98 MPa, and returns to the feed. Its up pass reaches the joint in channels 1.6 mm across, which is where that drop is spent. A fuel-rich gas generator at a mixture ratio of 0.35 and 10 MPa reads 968 K at equilibrium with 12 percent of its mass as graphite, which overstates the temperature a real generator reaches. Its turbine takes 669 kJ/kg at a pressure ratio of 16 and an efficiency of 0.6, and the 5 kg/s of exhaust enters at 775 K through a 15 mm slot at the circuit's far end. Under that film the C103 shell from twelve to the exit peaks at 1340 K, 333 K inside its limit. The jacket and the returned extension circuit draw 32 kg/s of the 33.6 kg/s of methane the chamber burns. Counting the exhaust as flow beside the chamber and its thrust kept apart from the core, the engine delivers 321.8 s at its design back pressure (324.7 s if the film mixes fully). Where D is viable: first stages and long bells where the throat must see cold coolant. Heritage for the split: SP-8087 names bifurcated tubes operational on the Titan II first stage and the F-1.",
